The interval extends three standard deviations on each side of the mean.
$P(\mu-3\sigma<X<\mu+3\sigma)\approx0.997$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.9cm,y=0.8cm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{region}{HTML}{D4E8FF}
\fill[region] (-3,0) -- plot[domain=-3:3,samples=80,variable=\x] ({\x},{2.5*exp(-\x*\x/2)}) -- (3,0) -- cycle;
\draw[black,thick] plot[domain=-3.7:3.7,samples=100,variable=\x] ({\x},{2.5*exp(-\x*\x/2)});
\draw[black,->] (-4,0)--(4,0);
\draw[black,dashed] (-3,0)--(-3,{2.5*exp(-9/2)});
\draw[black,dashed] (3,0)--(3,{2.5*exp(-9/2)});
\draw[black] (-3,0)--(-3,-0.12) (0,0)--(0,-0.12) (3,0)--(3,-0.12);
\path (-3,-0.12) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\mu-3\sigma$} (0,-0.12) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\mu$} (3,-0.12) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\mu+3\sigma$};
\path (0,1) node {$99.7\%$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Symmetry divides the central $95\%$ equally between the two sides of the mean.
$\begin{aligned}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}95\%\div2\\&=47.5\%\end{aligned}$
So $P(\mu<X<\mu+2\sigma)\approx0.475$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.9cm,y=0.8cm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{region}{HTML}{D4E8FF}
\fill[region] (0,0) -- plot[domain=0:2,samples=60,variable=\x] ({\x},{2.5*exp(-\x*\x/2)}) -- (2,0) -- cycle;
\draw[black,thick] plot[domain=-3.7:3.7,samples=100,variable=\x] ({\x},{2.5*exp(-\x*\x/2)});
\draw[black,->] (-4,0)--(4,0);
\draw[black,dashed] (0,0)--(0,2.5) (2,0)--(2,{2.5*exp(-2)});
\draw[black] (0,0)--(0,-0.12) (2,0)--(2,-0.12);
\path (0,-0.12) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\mu$} (2,-0.12) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\mu+2\sigma$};
\path (0.8,0.8) node {$47.5\%$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

About $99.7\%$ lies within three standard deviations. Divide the remaining percentage equally between the two tails.
$\begin{aligned}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}(100\%-99.7\%)\div2\\&=0.3\%\div2\\&=0.15\%\end{aligned}$
So $P(X<\mu-3\sigma)\approx0.0015$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.9cm,y=0.8cm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{region}{HTML}{D4E8FF}
\fill[region] (-3.7,0) -- plot[domain=-3.7:-3,samples=40,variable=\x] ({\x},{2.5*exp(-\x*\x/2)}) -- (-3,0) -- cycle;
\draw[black,thick] plot[domain=-3.7:3.7,samples=100,variable=\x] ({\x},{2.5*exp(-\x*\x/2)});
\draw[black,->] (-4,0)--(4,0);
\draw[black,dashed] (-3,0)--(-3,{2.5*exp(-9/2)});
\draw[black] (-3,0)--(-3,-0.12) (0,0)--(0,-0.12);
\path (-3,-0.12) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\mu-3\sigma$} (0,-0.12) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\mu$};
\draw[black,->] (-2.8,0.85)--(-3.35,0.03);
\path (-2.8,0.85) node[above] {$0.15\%$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Subtract the region from the mean to two standard deviations from the region from the mean to three standard deviations.
$\begin{aligned}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}99.7\%\div2-95\%\div2\\&=49.85\%-47.5\%\\&=2.35\%\end{aligned}$
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.9cm,y=0.8cm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{region}{HTML}{D4E8FF}
\fill[region] (2,0) -- plot[domain=2:3,samples=40,variable=\x] ({\x},{2.5*exp(-\x*\x/2)}) -- (3,0) -- cycle;
\draw[black,thick] plot[domain=-3.7:3.7,samples=100,variable=\x] ({\x},{2.5*exp(-\x*\x/2)});
\draw[black,->] (-4,0)--(4,0);
\draw[black,dashed] (2,0)--(2,{2.5*exp(-2)}) (3,0)--(3,{2.5*exp(-9/2)});
\draw[black] (0,0)--(0,-0.12) (2,0)--(2,-0.12) (3,0)--(3,-0.12);
\path (0,-0.12) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\mu$};
\path (2,-0.12) node[below,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont] {$\mu+2\sigma$};
\draw[black,thin] (3,-0.12)--(3,-0.95);
\path (3,-0.95) node[below,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont] {$\mu+3\sigma$};
\draw[black,->] (2.6,0.9)--(2.45,0.08);
\path (2.6,0.9) node[above] {$2.35\%$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Include the $50\%$ above the mean and the half-band between one standard deviation below the mean and the mean.
$\begin{aligned}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}50\%+68\%\div2\\&=50\%+34\%\\&=84\%\end{aligned}$
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.9cm,y=0.8cm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{region}{HTML}{D4E8FF}
\fill[region] (-1,0) -- plot[domain=-1:3.7,samples=80,variable=\x] ({\x},{2.5*exp(-\x*\x/2)}) -- (3.7,0) -- cycle;
\draw[black,thick] plot[domain=-3.7:3.7,samples=100,variable=\x] ({\x},{2.5*exp(-\x*\x/2)});
\draw[black,->] (-4,0)--(4,0);
\draw[black,dashed] (-1,0)--(-1,{2.5*exp(-0.5)}) (0,0)--(0,2.5);
\draw[black] (-1,0)--(-1,-0.12) (0,0)--(0,-0.12);
\path (-1,-0.12) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\mu-\sigma$} (0,-0.12) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\mu$};
\path (-0.5,0.65) node {$34\%$} (1,0.65) node {$50\%$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Split the interval at the mean and add the two half-bands.
$\begin{aligned}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}68\%\div2+99.7\%\div2\\&=34\%+49.85\%\\&=83.85\%\end{aligned}$
So $P(\mu-\sigma<X<\mu+3\sigma)\approx0.8385$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.9cm,y=0.8cm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{region}{HTML}{D4E8FF}
\fill[region] (-1,0) -- plot[domain=-1:3,samples=80,variable=\x] ({\x},{2.5*exp(-\x*\x/2)}) -- (3,0) -- cycle;
\draw[black,thick] plot[domain=-3.7:3.7,samples=100,variable=\x] ({\x},{2.5*exp(-\x*\x/2)});
\draw[black,->] (-4,0)--(4,0);
\draw[black,dashed] (-1,0)--(-1,{2.5*exp(-0.5)}) (0,0)--(0,2.5) (3,0)--(3,{2.5*exp(-9/2)});
\draw[black] (-1,0)--(-1,-0.12) (0,0)--(0,-0.12) (3,0)--(3,-0.12);
\path (-1,-0.12) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\mu-\sigma$} (0,-0.12) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\mu$} (3,-0.12) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\mu+3\sigma$};
\path (-0.5,0.65) node {$34\%$} (1.1,0.65) node {$49.85\%$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

(a) $56=64-2(4)$ and $72=64+2(4)$. Both boundaries are two standard deviations from the mean, so approximately $95\%$ of scores lie between them.

(b) $52=64-3(4)$ and $68=64+4$. The interval extends three standard deviations below the mean and one above it.
$\begin{aligned}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}99.7\%\div2+68\%\div2\\&=49.85\%+34\%\\&=83.85\%\end{aligned}$

(c) $56$ is two standard deviations below the mean.
$\begin{aligned}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}(100\%-95\%)\div2\\&=2.5\%\end{aligned}$

The marked scores are equally spaced by one standard deviation.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.9cm,y=0.8cm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[black,thick] plot[domain=-3.7:3.7,samples=100,variable=\x] ({\x},{2.5*exp(-\x*\x/2)});
\draw[black,->] (-4,0)--(4,0);
\foreach \u/\v in {-3/52,-2/56,-1/60,0/64,1/68,2/72,3/76}{
\draw[black,dashed] (\u,0)--(\u,{2.5*exp(-\u*\u/2)});
\draw[black] (\u,0)--(\u,-0.12);
\path (\u,-0.12) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};
}
\path (0,-0.7) node {Score};
\draw[black,<->] (-3,2.9)--(1,2.9);
\path (-1,2.9) node[above] {(b) $83.85\%$};
\draw[black,<->] (-2,3.65)--(2,3.65);
\path (0,3.65) node[above] {(a) $95\%$};
\draw[black,->] (-2.8,1.1)--(-2.65,0.04);
\path (-2.8,1.1) node[above] {(c) $2.5\%$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$54=42+3(4)$, so the time is three standard deviations above the mean.
$\begin{aligned}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}(100\%-99.7\%)\div2\\&=0.15\%\end{aligned}$
Only about $0.15\%$ of cycles take at least this long, so $54$ seconds is unusually long.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.9cm,y=0.8cm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{region}{HTML}{D4E8FF}
\fill[region] (3,0) -- plot[domain=3:3.7,samples=40,variable=\x] ({\x},{2.5*exp(-\x*\x/2)}) -- (3.7,0) -- cycle;
\draw[black,thick] plot[domain=-3.7:3.7,samples=100,variable=\x] ({\x},{2.5*exp(-\x*\x/2)});
\draw[black,->] (-4,0)--(4,0);
\draw[black,dashed] (3,0)--(3,{2.5*exp(-9/2)});
\draw[black] (0,0)--(0,-0.12) (3,0)--(3,-0.12);
\path (0,-0.12) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$42$} (3,-0.12) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$54$};
\path (0,-0.7) node {Time (seconds)};
\draw[black,->] (2.7,0.85)--(3.35,0.03);
\path (2.7,0.85) node[above] {$0.15\%$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
